WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.458 --> 00:00:04.916
Hello! Welcome to another episode of Bug
 Bounty Eeports Explained.

00:00:05.166 --> 00:00:10.791
Today, I will show you how Tom Van Goethem
 was able to leak parts of undisclosed

00:00:10.791 --> 00:00:15.125
Hackerone reports by using a novel
 method of cross-site leaks.

00:00:15.625 --> 00:00:19.333
Make sure to watch the video
 until the end, because I will show

00:00:19.333 --> 00:00:23.458
the outstanding trick, which
 he used to have a little bit of control

00:00:23.541 --> 00:00:26.000
over TCP protocol from the browser.

00:00:26.458 --> 00:00:30.083
Links to the report
 and to his media are in the description.

00:00:30.208 --> 00:00:35.791
Enjoy! On Hackerone,
 there is a /bugs.json endpoint.

00:00:36.041 --> 00:00:40.041
It's used under the hood
 when you use the search feature on inbox.

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:45.291
It has text_query parameter,
 which you can use to query all the bugs

00:00:45.416 --> 00:00:49.916
to which you have access
 to as a reporter, triager or whoever else.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:55.083
The endpoint used the GET method
 and no CSRF protection.

00:00:55.958 --> 00:00:58.750
As the attacker,
 you were able to send requests

00:00:58.750 --> 00:01:02.125
to this endpoint
 if a victim visited your website.

00:01:02.958 --> 00:01:07.875
Same origin policy would allow you
 to query reports to which the victim

00:01:07.875 --> 00:01:12.041
had access to and cookies
 would be attached to the request.

00:01:12.958 --> 00:01:17.791
However, same origin policy
 would not allow you to read the response,

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:21.916
so you can't actually disclose
 report contents this way.

00:01:22.875 --> 00:01:27.250
But there are methods to exfiltrate data
 in such a scenario.

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:30.500
Those attacks are called cross-site leaks.

00:01:31.625 --> 00:01:34.708
The most common approach
 is looking at the timing.

00:01:34.958 --> 00:01:40.583
It works this way: the victim visits
 your website, your JavaScript, sends

00:01:40.583 --> 00:01:44.333
two requests, one with searching a string

00:01:44.458 --> 00:01:47.458
that will never be present in any report.

00:01:48.458 --> 00:01:51.708
The other one with a string
 that you want to confirm

00:01:51.708 --> 00:01:56.250
if it's inside an undisclosed report
 to which the victim has access.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:01.500
When one response will return your results
 while the other will not,

00:02:01.750 --> 00:02:06.166
the processing time of the one with 
 reports will be longer.

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:10.041
You can measure the response
 time of each request,

00:02:10.041 --> 00:02:12.625
even if you can't read
 the response itself.

00:02:13.416 --> 00:02:16.166
So if one was longer than the other,

00:02:16.458 --> 00:02:21.125
you know the string is present
 somewhere in the undisclosed report.

00:02:21.958 --> 00:02:25.125
If both responses
 had roughly the same time,

00:02:25.291 --> 00:02:28.916
that means your string is not present
 in any of the reports.

00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:35.458
This way, you could brute force parts of
 reports contents, one character at a time.

00:02:36.791 --> 00:02:39.750
But it's not as easy as I made it look.

00:02:40.916 --> 00:02:44.833
Differences in the time that the server
 will take to process

00:02:44.875 --> 00:02:50.416
such a simple operation are measured
 in microseconds or even in nanoseconds.

00:02:51.083 --> 00:02:53.458
Considering that the network delay

00:02:53.458 --> 00:02:57.875
is order of magnitude
 higher, in milliseconds, the difference

00:02:57.875 --> 00:03:01.083
from a single request
 doesn't mean anything.

00:03:01.875 --> 00:03:05.291
However, when you send many parts
 of such requests,

00:03:05.541 --> 00:03:09.708
you can measure averages
 and come up with significant differences.

00:03:10.541 --> 00:03:15.000
Behind this report, there's
 a bigger reseach regarding these attacks.

00:03:15.583 --> 00:03:20.541
Researchers determined that depending
 on the network location and processing

00:03:20.541 --> 00:03:24.958
time, you must send hundreds
 or thousands of requests to get

00:03:24.958 --> 00:03:29.666
meaningful averages, which will tell you
 if you did leak something or not.

00:03:30.875 --> 00:03:35.583
And that's only to tell
 if one character is in one position.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:41.291
You'd have to send thousands of requests
 to tell if A is the next character,

00:03:41.458 --> 00:03:46.416
then thousands of requests to tell
 if B is the next character and so on.

00:03:47.416 --> 00:03:49.500
Exfiltrating even a short

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:52.583
token would require millions of requests.

00:03:53.333 --> 00:03:55.833
And this is a client-site attack.

00:03:55.958 --> 00:04:00.208
So the victim would have to
 stay on your website for this whole time,

00:04:01.333 --> 00:04:04.583
not to even mention
 rate limits on Hackerone.

00:04:05.250 --> 00:04:08.000
So the time attack wouldn't work here.

00:04:09.083 --> 00:04:11.458
Take a look at that from this perspective.

00:04:11.916 --> 00:04:15.083
You send two requests
 and the time difference

00:04:15.125 --> 00:04:18.208
you're interested in
 is here, on the server.

00:04:18.958 --> 00:04:22.000
But the place you try
 to measure it is here.

00:04:22.375 --> 00:04:24.666
Many routers and switches away.

00:04:25.916 --> 00:04:27.875
But it has to be that way. 

00:04:27.875 --> 00:04:31.458
You can't measure the time
 directly on the server.

00:04:33.041 --> 00:04:37.416
But actually, you are not interested
 in the time it took.

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:42.541
You are interested
 in which of these two took longer?

00:04:43.708 --> 00:04:49.541
Researchers idea was to see which of these
 two responses would come back earlier.

00:04:50.791 --> 00:04:53.708
So the hunter would send two requests.

00:04:54.875 --> 00:04:57.541
When both where it would yield no results,

00:04:57.750 --> 00:05:00.958
there was about 50 percent chance response

00:05:00.958 --> 00:05:03.791
1 would come back after response 2.

00:05:04.625 --> 00:05:08.166
However, when request
 one would return a bug,

00:05:08.375 --> 00:05:12.583
there was about 80 percent chance
 it would come back second.

00:05:13.750 --> 00:05:19.208
This still doesn't allow you to determine
 the character after one pair of requests,

00:05:19.208 --> 00:05:25.208
but this time, instead of hundreds
 or thousands, 20 requests was enough.

00:05:25.833 --> 00:05:29.291
And your location
 or your connection didn't matter.

00:05:30.416 --> 00:05:36.000
For that to work, he would have to lock
 the order of responses at this point

00:05:36.041 --> 00:05:39.291
to make sure it's
 not affected on the road home.

00:05:40.166 --> 00:05:42.875
Let me show you how he achieved it.

00:05:42.875 --> 00:05:47.458
HTTP requests are transmitted
 in TCP packets.

00:05:48.375 --> 00:05:50.666
You can think of it as the letters.

00:05:51.375 --> 00:05:57.083
The HTTP request is the letter itself
 and TCP packet in the envelope.

00:05:57.708 --> 00:06:01.583
If your letter is long,
 it will be sent in multiple envelopes.

00:06:02.333 --> 00:06:05.708
But if you have two short
 letters to the same address,

00:06:05.708 --> 00:06:08.958
you can send both of them
 in a single envelope.

00:06:09.666 --> 00:06:13.333
So what do you need to do
 is to bundle both HTTP

00:06:13.333 --> 00:06:16.041
requests in a single TCP packet.

00:06:16.875 --> 00:06:21.208
In case of an attack against the server,
 without the client interaction,

00:06:21.208 --> 00:06:24.458
this is easy-peasy
 because you can write a low level

00:06:24.458 --> 00:06:26.875
script that will do whatever you want.

00:06:27.625 --> 00:06:30.541
But in this attack, we are in the browser

00:06:30.541 --> 00:06:34.666
and we have little control over low level
 network protocols.

00:06:35.333 --> 00:06:40.458
How do you then force the browser
 to send both letters in a single envelope?

00:06:41.666 --> 00:06:46.875
The browser has built in mechanism
 to prevent sending too many envelopes

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:50.541
before the server acknowledges
 that they were received.

00:06:51.583 --> 00:06:54.416
So the server will send as many envelopes

00:06:54.416 --> 00:06:59.458
as it can and the rest will be stored
 in the queue, waiting for the server

00:06:59.458 --> 00:07:02.416
to acknowledge receiving
 those sent envelopes.

00:07:03.208 --> 00:07:08.708
Normally, the browser would envelope
 the first HTTP request straight away

00:07:08.875 --> 00:07:11.041
without waiting for the second one.

00:07:11.833 --> 00:07:16.625
But when your two letters will be queued,
 they will be packed in a single envelope,

00:07:16.791 --> 00:07:18.958
which is exactly what we need.

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:23.625
But how do you force the browser
 to queue your requests?

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:27.000
By sending a big HTTP request first,

00:07:27.041 --> 00:07:30.083
which will be too big
 to be sent in one go.

00:07:30.916 --> 00:07:35.541
Part of the request will be sent,
 but the remaining packets will be queued.

00:07:36.208 --> 00:07:39.625
Immediately after you send
 your pair of requests.

00:07:39.791 --> 00:07:44.333
One with the baseline query and the one
 with the query you are interested in.

00:07:44.958 --> 00:07:48.416
Both of them will be added
 to the end of the queue.

00:07:49.375 --> 00:07:52.250
After the sender acknowledges,
 that it received

00:07:52.250 --> 00:07:56.291
the first batch of packets, ones from
 the queue will be sent

00:07:56.291 --> 00:08:01.708
and your pair of HTTP requests
 will be bundled into a one TCP packet.

00:08:02.541 --> 00:08:05.333
Then you repeat the operation 20 times

00:08:05.333 --> 00:08:09.416
and you look how often the response with
 the query was second.

00:08:10.500 --> 00:08:13.666
If it was second
 in about half of requests,

00:08:14.333 --> 00:08:17.541
your query did not return any reports.

00:08:17.541 --> 00:08:22.666
If it was second in about 16 requests,
 that means that the string

00:08:22.708 --> 00:08:27.625
you are looking for is present
 in at least one of the victims reports.

00:08:28.750 --> 00:08:32.416
You can move on to the next character
 or the next word.

00:08:33.458 --> 00:08:36.416
This way, it was possible to exfiltrate

00:08:36.541 --> 00:08:39.625
parts of private reports from Hackerone.

00:08:39.625 --> 00:08:44.875
Still, even this optimized
 technique requires a lot of requests.

00:08:45.416 --> 00:08:49.833
The reporter estimated about 1,5 to 3,5

00:08:49.833 --> 00:08:53.916
minutes per character,
 assuming you know the first two.

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:58.166
This way, you could target, for example,
 authorization headers,

00:08:58.750 --> 00:09:04.125
some endpoints, specific vulnerability
 classes or whatever comes to your mind.

00:09:05.083 --> 00:09:09.750
But whatever you chose, the attack
 would still probably take a few hours.

00:09:10.875 --> 00:09:13.291
So by no means this is a high risk bug

00:09:13.291 --> 00:09:16.541
but Hackerone staff
 wrote in the comments

00:09:16.541 --> 00:09:20.333
that there were about 8 reports
 about this behavior,

00:09:20.666 --> 00:09:24.791
and this was the first one
 that presented a feasible attack.

00:09:24.916 --> 00:09:28.791
Hence, this one was awarded $2,500.

00:09:29.625 --> 00:09:35.375
As for the fix, the /bugs.json endpoint
 now uses the POST method and thus,

00:09:35.416 --> 00:09:40.083
it requires a CSRF token,
 making this attack impossible.

00:09:40.666 --> 00:09:43.333
And that's it. I'm always a bit sad

00:09:43.333 --> 00:09:47.125
when the complex bug doesn't
 have the critical impact.

00:09:47.458 --> 00:09:52.375
But anyway, I learned a lot while
 studying this report, and I hope you, too.

00:09:52.708 --> 00:09:56.666
If you did make sure to feed the YouTube
 algorithm with a like

00:09:56.666 --> 00:09:59.583
so that it recommends
 the video to other hackers.

00:10:00.041 --> 00:10:03.708
I also encourage you
 to watch the whole presentation about this

00:10:03.708 --> 00:10:07.083
timeless technique
 where you can also find Wi-Fi attacks.

00:10:07.291 --> 00:10:09.791
The video is on the card right now.

00:10:10.208 --> 00:10:12.708
For now, thank you
 for watching and goodbye.

